course: medicine
lesson: 51
title: Water, fizzy drinks and sports drinks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: About 60% of a child's body is water, so a 30 kg child carries around 18 kg of it, and every drop of sweat on a hot run is some of that water leaving.
explain: Lose too much and you get thirsty, headachy, tired and slow, like Oliver in Leeds who skipped the water table, «Nah, I'm not nesh, me» («nesh» is Northern for soft about cold or hardship), and finished grey-faced with a pounding head.
explain: Small sips before, during and after sport top you up; gulping two whole bottles at once just sloshes about in your tummy.
explain: A can of cola has about 35 g of sugar, more than the whole day's limit for a child of 7 to 10, and sports drinks were made for adults doing long hard exercise, so for a kids' match plain tap water does the job.
fact: Sweat is water leaving the body, and losing too much makes you thirsty, tired, headachy and slower.
fact: UK advice is about 6 to 8 cups or glasses of fluid a day, with more on hot days and sport days; water, milk and no-sugar drinks all count.
fact: Pee colour is a simple check: pale straw means you've drunk enough, and dark yellow often means you need more water.
fact: Swimmers sweat too, even though they don't notice because they are wet, so they need drinks at the poolside.
fact: In the UK, Australia and many other countries tap water is tested and safe, and it hydrates just as well as bottled water.
fact: The UK daily limit of free sugars for children aged 7 to 10 is about 24 g, about 6 sugar cubes of roughly 4 g each.
fact: A 330 ml can of regular cola has about 35 g of sugar, about 9 cubes, more than that whole daily limit.
fact: Fizz is gas, so gulping fizzy drinks fills the stomach with bubbles and can cause burping and a stitch.
fact: Sports drinks were designed for long, hard exercise; for a child playing for under an hour, water is enough and the extra sugar is just extra sugar.
fact: Sports drinks are sugary and acidic, so sipping them slowly for hours bathes the teeth again and again and can cause decay.
fact: UK advice is no more than 150 ml of fruit juice or smoothie a day, ideally with a meal, and juice counts as only one of your 5 a day however much you drink.
fact: Drinking huge amounts at once before a game makes you sloshy and sick; drink a glass or so before and sip during.
fact: «Necks» in British slang means drinks very fast in one go.
exercise: About how much of a child's body is water? | About 60% | About 6% | About 99% | None of it
exercise: What is sweat? | Water leaving the body to cool it | Fat melting out of the muscles | Extra blood leaking through the skin | Germs being pushed out
exercise: How much should children drink a day, by UK advice? | About 6 to 8 cups or glasses | About 1 small glass for the whole day | About 30 big glasses, or even more | Only when you feel very thirsty
exercise: Which drinks count towards your daily fluid? | Water, milk and no-sugar drinks | Only bottled mineral water | Only sports drinks and energy drinks | Only fizzy drinks
exercise: Your pee is dark yellow after rugby. What does it suggest? | You probably need to drink more water | You drank far too much | You ate far too many carrots at lunchtime | You're fully hydrated
exercise: Can you get dehydrated while swimming? | Yes, swimmers sweat too | No, the water soaks into you | No, pools are too cold | Only in the sea
exercise: Is tap water in the UK as good as bottled? | Yes, it's tested, safe and hydrates as well | No, bottled is always healthier | No, tap water has no water in it | Only if you boil it first
exercise: What's the daily free sugar limit for a child of 7 to 10? | About 24 g, about 6 cubes | About 100 g, about 25 cubes | About 2 g, half a cube | No limit for sporty kids
exercise: How much sugar is in a regular can of cola? | About 35 g, about 9 cubes | About 3 g, less than a cube | No sugar, it's just fizz | About 200 g, a whole bag
exercise: Why might cola at half-time give you a stitch? | The gas and gulping fill your stomach | Cola cools the muscles down far too much | Cola has no actual water in it, not a drop | The sugar freezes into a lump in your side
exercise: Do children need sports drinks for an ordinary match? | No, water is enough for under an hour | Yes, they make kids play much better and faster | Yes, every match needs one | Only if the team wins
exercise: Why are sports drinks sipped for hours bad for teeth? | They're sugary and acidic and bathe the teeth | They're too cold for the teeth | They make teeth grow too fast | They wash the toothpaste off
exercise: How much fruit juice a day is advised at most? | About 150 ml, ideally with a meal | About 2 litres, because it's fruit | As much as you like, whenever you like | None at all, ever, not even a small glass
exercise: Why not drink two big bottles right before a game? | It sloshes about and can make you sick | You'll be too heavy to jump | The water won't reach your muscles in time | Water before sport is banned
test: Oliver's not stopping at the water table: «not nesh, me». How does he end up? | Thirsty, headachy and slower | Faster, because he's lighter | Stronger from toughing it out | Exactly the same
test: Chloe sneers at Ava's tap water: «bottled is way healthier, no cap». Right? | No, safe tap water hydrates just as well | Yes, tap water makes you ill | Yes, bottled has extra energy and vitamins in it | No, tap water has more sugar
test: Aarav: «in the pool all morning innit, can't get thirsty in water». Dry lips by lunch. Why? | He still sweated in the warm pool | Pool water made his lips go all dry | He drank way too much of the pool water | Swimmers never need drinks, so it's weird
test: Grace's dad hands out cola at half-time, «that'll give you wings». What would be better? | Water | A second can | A coffee | An energy drink
test: Jack: «35 g of sugar, grand, that's nothing». Niamh: «eat nine sugar cubes then, eejit». Who's right? | Niamh, a can is more than the day's sugar limit | Jack, sugar in drinks doesn't count | Jack, 35 g is only one cube | Neither, cola has no sugar
test: Lily wants a blue sports drink for a 40-minute tennis match, «it's giving champion». What's best? | Water; the sports drink adds sugar, not skill | The sports drink will make her play much better | Two sports drinks for double power | Cola instead, it's cheaper
test: Ethan sips a sports drink for a 3-hour tournament. What will the dentist worry about? | Tooth decay from all that sugary sipping | His teeth being too clean | Nothing, because sports drinks are really healthy | That he drank too much water
test: Ruby: «how much am I meant to drink, like a bucket?» What's the answer? | About 6 to 8 drinks a day, more when hot or active | A whole bucket every day | One small glass a day | Only when she feels faint
test: Callum's pee is «dead dark, like tea» after rugby. His mum says? | Drink some water, his body is telling him | That's normal, ignore it | He's had too much water | He should stop drinking anything for a whole day
test: Thabo necks two bottles before kick-off, «lekker, loaded up». Ten minutes in? | Sloshing and feeling sick | Running faster than ever | Never thirsty again for a week | Floating across the pitch
test: Nan: «it's fruit, love», and Mia drinks 500 ml of orange juice. What's the problem? | It's over three times the 150 ml daily limit | Nothing, juice is as good as fruit | Juice has no sugar in it | 500 ml is the exact daily amount
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the consumption of fluid is recommended during physical exertion. | To replace water lost through perspiration | To increase the weight of the body for greater strength | To cool the stomach exclusively | To dilute the blood to excess
exam: Pursuant to guidance issued in the United Kingdom, determine the maximum daily quantity of free sugars recommended for a child aged between seven and ten years. | Approximately twenty-four grams | Approximately two hundred grams | Approximately two grams | No maximum is prescribed
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the use of sports drinks by children engaged in play of less than one hour. | Water is sufficient and the additional sugar confers no benefit | Sports drinks are indispensable to adequate performance | Water is harmful in such circumstances | Sports drinks prevent dental decay
exam: Ascertain the significance of dark yellow urine following exercise. | It frequently indicates the need for additional water | It indicates excessive consumption of water | It indicates a fracture of the bone | It is of no significance whatsoever
